/* Pathology 11–12: Respiratory, GIT, salivary gland, eye */
Z("path11",0,"p",["ARDS is sudden, severe lung failure where the tiny air sacs fill with protein-rich fluid and hyaline membranes because the alveolar–capillary wall is damaged.","Like a sponge whose walls are punctured and soaked so it can no longer hold air.",["Causes: sepsis, pneumonia, aspiration, trauma, shock, pancreatitis, transfusion.","Diffuse alveolar damage: hyaline membranes are the hallmark.","Severe hypoxaemia not corrected by oxygen; bilateral infiltrates ('white-out').","Not due to heart failure (normal wedge pressure)."],"ARDS = 'Alveoli Rained with protein, Damaged & Stiff'."],
"Acute respiratory distress syndrome is acute diffuse alveolar–capillary injury causing non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema, rapid-onset severe hypoxaemia refractory to oxygen and bilateral radiographic infiltrates. Causes: sepsis, diffuse pulmonary infections (pneumonia, COVID-19), gastric aspiration, major trauma, shock, acute pancreatitis, fat embolism, toxic gases and transfusion. Neutrophils and cytokines (IL-8, TNF) damage endothelium and type I pneumocytes. Morphology: heavy, red, airless lungs; diffuse alveolar damage with hyaline membranes (exudative phase), followed by type II pneumocyte proliferation and interstitial fibrosis (organising phase).",
[["Pathogenesis",["Injury to endothelium or epithelium → macrophages release IL-1, IL-8, TNF","Neutrophils sequestered → proteases, ROS → further damage","↑ Permeability → protein-rich oedema, loss of surfactant","Hyaline membranes (fibrin + necrotic cell debris) line alveolar ducts","Imbalance of pro- and anti-inflammatory mediators"]],
["Morphology",["Acute (exudative): heavy, firm, red lungs; congestion, oedema, hyaline membranes","Organising: type II pneumocyte hyperplasia, interstitial fibrosis","Most recover; some develop diffuse fibrosis"]],
["Clinical",["Dyspnoea, tachypnoea, cyanosis within 72 h of insult","PaO2/FiO2 ≤ 300 (Berlin definition)","Bilateral opacities; no left heart failure","Mortality 30–40%"]]],
{c:"ARDS is a final common pathway of severe lung injury; supportive lung-protective ventilation and treating the trigger are the main therapy."});

Z("path11",1,"p",["Lobar pneumonia is infection that fills an entire lobe of the lung with inflammatory exudate, usually caused by pneumococcus.","The lobe turns from a light sponge into a solid liver-like block as it fills with inflammatory fluid.",["Organism: Streptococcus pneumoniae (90%).","Four stages: congestion, red hepatisation, grey hepatisation, resolution.","Features: sudden fever, rigors, pleuritic pain, rusty sputum.","Complications: empyema, lung abscess, bacteraemia, meningitis."],"Stages 'CRGR': Congestion, Red, Grey, Resolution."],
"Lobar pneumonia is an acute bacterial infection producing consolidation of a large part or whole of a lobe, mostly caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae (also Klebsiella in alcoholics). The classic four stages are: congestion (day 1–2: vascular engorgement, oedema fluid with bacteria), red hepatisation (day 2–4: alveoli filled with neutrophils, RBCs and fibrin; lobe red, firm, liver-like), grey hepatisation (day 4–8: RBCs lyse, fibrinosuppurative exudate persists; grey-brown dry surface) and resolution (day 8–9: enzymatic digestion of exudate, restoration of architecture). Clinical: sudden high fever, chills, pleuritic chest pain, productive rusty sputum, bronchial breathing. Complications: abscess, empyema, organisation (carnification), bacteraemic spread.",
[["Stages",[["Stage","Time","Gross","Microscopy"],["Congestion","Day 1–2","Heavy, boggy, red","Vascular congestion, oedema fluid, bacteria, few neutrophils"],["Red hepatisation","Day 2–4","Red, firm, airless, liver-like","Alveoli packed with neutrophils, RBCs, fibrin"],["Grey hepatisation","Day 4–8","Grey-brown, dry","RBCs lysed, fibrin and neutrophils persist"],["Resolution","Day 8 onwards","Normal","Exudate digested, removed by macrophages"]]],
["Lobar vs bronchopneumonia",[["Feature","Lobar","Bronchopneumonia"],["Distribution","Whole lobe","Patchy, around bronchioles, bilateral basal"],["Organism","Pneumococcus","Staph, H. influenzae, Klebsiella"],["Age","Adults","Extremes of age"]]],
["Complications",["Lung abscess, empyema","Organisation → carnification (fibrous)","Bacteraemia → meningitis, endocarditis, arthritis"]]],
{c:"Classic lobar pneumonia evolves through four predictable stages and, with antibiotics, usually resolves without scarring."});

Z("path11",2,"p",["COPD is a long-term lung disease with airflow obstruction that does not fully reverse, mainly emphysema and chronic bronchitis, usually caused by smoking.","Emphysema is like a sponge with its walls torn into large holes; chronic bronchitis is like pipes clogged with mucus.",["Smoking is the main cause; also biomass fuel smoke and α1-antitrypsin deficiency.","Emphysema: destruction of alveolar walls → 'pink puffers'.","Chronic bronchitis: cough with sputum ≥ 3 months for 2 consecutive years → 'blue bloaters'.","FEV1/FVC < 0.7 after bronchodilator."],"COPD = 'Emphysema destroys walls; Bronchitis blocks with mucus'."],
"Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is a common, preventable disease characterised by persistent respiratory symptoms and airflow limitation (post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC < 0.7) due to airway and/or alveolar abnormalities, usually from significant exposure to noxious particles or gases — mainly cigarette smoke, and in India, biomass fuel smoke. It includes emphysema (permanent enlargement of airspaces distal to terminal bronchioles with wall destruction, no obvious fibrosis) and chronic bronchitis (persistent productive cough for at least 3 months in each of 2 consecutive years). Other obstructive diseases are asthma, bronchiectasis and small airway disease. Pathogenesis involves inflammation (neutrophils, macrophages, CD8 T cells), protease–antiprotease imbalance and oxidative stress. Complications: cor pulmonale, respiratory failure, pneumothorax from bullae.",
[["Definition and spectrum","COPD = emphysema + chronic bronchitis (frequently coexist) with irreversible airflow obstruction. Related obstructive diseases: asthma (reversible), bronchiectasis, bronchiolitis."],
["Types of emphysema",[["Type","Region of acinus","Location","Association"],["Centriacinar (centrilobular)","Respiratory bronchioles (central)","Upper lobes","Smoking (95%)"],["Panacinar","Entire acinus","Lower lobes","α1-antitrypsin deficiency"],["Distal acinar (paraseptal)","Distal near pleura","Upper, subpleural","Spontaneous pneumothorax in young"],["Irregular","Irregular","Around scars","Healed inflammation"]]],
["Pathogenesis",["Smoke → macrophages and neutrophils recruited (IL-8, LTB4, TNF)","Neutrophil elastase and macrophage MMPs destroy elastic tissue; α1-antitrypsin inactivated by oxidants (protease–antiprotease imbalance)","Oxidative stress from smoke and inflammatory cells","Chronic bronchitis: mucus gland hypertrophy, goblet cell metaplasia, small airway fibrosis","Loss of elastic recoil → airways collapse on expiration → air trapping"]],
["Morphology",["Emphysema: voluminous pale lungs overlapping heart; large apical bullae; enlarged airspaces, thin walls, loss of capillaries","Chronic bronchitis: hyperplasia of submucosal mucus glands (Reid index > 0.4), goblet cell metaplasia, squamous metaplasia, bronchiolitis obliterans"]],
["Pink puffer vs blue bloater",[["Feature","Emphysema (pink puffer)","Chronic bronchitis (blue bloater)"],["Build","Thin, barrel chest","Obese, oedematous"],["Dyspnoea","Early, severe","Late"],["Cough/sputum","Mild","Prominent"],["Cyanosis","Absent","Marked"],["Blood gases","Near-normal","Hypoxia, hypercapnia"],["Cor pulmonale","Late","Early"]]]],
{i:"COPD is now one of the top three causes of death worldwide; in India indoor air pollution from cooking fuels is an important cause in non-smokers, especially women.",x:[["Investigations",["Spirometry: ↓ FEV1, FEV1/FVC < 0.7, little reversibility","↑ TLC and RV (air trapping), ↓ DLCO in emphysema","X-ray: hyperinflation, flat diaphragm, bullae","α1-antitrypsin level in young patients"]],["Complications",["Cor pulmonale and right heart failure","Respiratory failure; acute exacerbations with infection","Pneumothorax, secondary polycythaemia"]]],f:"Smoke / biomass → macrophages + neutrophils\n → elastase & MMPs + oxidants (↓ α1-AT)\n ├ alveolar wall destruction → emphysema → loss of recoil\n └ mucus gland hyperplasia, airway fibrosis → chronic bronchitis\n → airflow obstruction → hypoxia → pulmonary hypertension → cor pulmonale",c:"COPD is largely preventable; stopping smoking and reducing biomass smoke exposure are the only measures that alter its course."});

Z("path11",3,"p",["Emphysema is permanent enlargement of the air spaces beyond the terminal bronchioles with destruction of their walls.","Many small soap bubbles merging into a few big ones — total surface for gas exchange is lost.",["Types: centriacinar (smokers, upper lobes), panacinar (α1-antitrypsin deficiency, lower lobes), paraseptal, irregular.","Protease–antiprotease imbalance destroys elastic tissue.","Pink puffer: thin, pursed-lip breathing, barrel chest.","Bullae may rupture → pneumothorax."],"Centri = Cigarettes (upper); Pan = Protease inhibitor deficiency (lower)."],
"Emphysema is a condition of the lung characterised by irreversible enlargement of the airspaces distal to the terminal bronchiole, accompanied by destruction of their walls without obvious fibrosis. Types by acinar distribution: centriacinar (respiratory bronchioles; upper lobes; smokers), panacinar (uniform involvement of the acinus; lower lobes; α1-antitrypsin deficiency), distal acinar/paraseptal (near pleura; spontaneous pneumothorax) and irregular (around scars). Pathogenesis: inhaled smoke recruits neutrophils and macrophages that release elastases and metalloproteinases, while oxidants inactivate α1-antitrypsin, destroying elastic tissue. Lungs are voluminous and pale with bullae. Clinically: progressive dyspnoea, barrel chest, prolonged expiration, pursed lips, weight loss ('pink puffer').",
[["Types",[["Type","Site","Cause"],["Centriacinar","Central respiratory bronchioles, upper lobes","Smoking"],["Panacinar","Whole acinus, lower lobes","α1-antitrypsin deficiency"],["Paraseptal","Distal, subpleural","Spontaneous pneumothorax"],["Irregular","Near scars","Healed lesions"]]],
["Pathogenesis",["Inflammatory cells → elastase, MMPs","↓ α1-antitrypsin (genetic PiZZ or oxidant inactivation)","Oxidative injury and apoptosis of alveolar cells","Loss of elastic recoil → expiratory collapse of small airways"]],
["Morphology",["Pale, voluminous lungs; bullae at apices","Enlarged alveoli, thin septa, 'floating' fragments of septa, loss of capillaries"]]],
{c:"Emphysema causes irreversible loss of gas-exchange surface; avoidance of smoke and α1-antitrypsin replacement in deficient patients slow progression."});

Z("path11",4,"p",["Bronchial asthma is a chronic inflammatory airway disease causing episodes of wheeze and breathlessness due to reversible narrowing of the airways.","Airways that are 'over-sensitive' — a speck of dust makes them tighten, swell and fill with sticky mucus.",["Atopic (extrinsic): Th2, IgE, eosinophils, allergens; children.","Non-atopic: infections, cold, exercise, aspirin.","Pathology: mucus plugs with Curschmann spirals, Charcot–Leyden crystals, thick basement membrane, smooth muscle hypertrophy.","Reversible obstruction: FEV1 improves > 12% after bronchodilator."],"Asthma sputum = 'Curschmann Curls, Charcot Crystals, Creola bodies'."],
"Bronchial asthma is a chronic inflammatory disorder of the airways causing recurrent episodes of wheezing, breathlessness, chest tightness and cough, especially at night, with reversible bronchoconstriction and airway hyperresponsiveness. Atopic asthma (most common) is a type I hypersensitivity: allergen-induced Th2 response, IL-4 (IgE), IL-5 (eosinophils), IL-13 (mucus); the immediate phase involves mast cell mediators (histamine, leukotrienes, PGD2) causing bronchospasm and mucus, and the late phase involves eosinophil and leucocyte recruitment. Non-atopic types are triggered by infections, pollutants, aspirin and occupational agents. Airway remodelling: thickened basement membrane, submucosal gland and smooth muscle hypertrophy, goblet cell hyperplasia, mucus plugs containing Curschmann spirals, eosinophils and Charcot–Leyden crystals. Status asthmaticus is a severe unrelenting attack.",
[["Types",["Atopic (extrinsic): family history, IgE, skin test positive","Non-atopic (intrinsic): viral infection, cold air","Drug-induced: aspirin (COX block → ↑ leukotrienes)","Occupational: fumes, wood dust","Exercise-induced"]],
["Pathogenesis (atopic)",["Sensitisation: allergen → Th2 cells → IL-4 (IgE), IL-5, IL-13","IgE binds mast cells; re-exposure → degranulation","Early phase (minutes): bronchoconstriction, mucus, vasodilatation","Late phase (hours): eosinophils, neutrophils, T cells → epithelial damage","Repeated attacks → airway remodelling"]],
["Morphology",["Lungs overdistended; occlusion of bronchi by thick mucus plugs","Curschmann spirals (shed epithelium in mucus), Charcot–Leyden crystals (eosinophil galectin-10), Creola bodies","Thick basement membrane, eosinophils, smooth muscle and gland hypertrophy"]]],
{c:"Asthma differs from COPD by its reversibility and eosinophilic inflammation; inhaled corticosteroids control the underlying inflammation."});

Z("path11",5,"p",["Bronchiectasis is permanent widening of bronchi due to destruction of their walls by repeated infection and obstruction.","Like a drainpipe that has been stretched out of shape — it fills with stagnant dirty water (pus) that keeps getting infected.",["Causes: post-infection (TB, measles, pertussis), cystic fibrosis, Kartagener syndrome, obstruction, ABPA.","Usually lower lobes, bilateral.","Copious foul-smelling purulent sputum, haemoptysis, clubbing.","HRCT: dilated bronchi ('signet ring', tram track)."],"Bronchiectasis = 'Big bronchi, Buckets of sputum'."],
"Bronchiectasis is permanent dilatation of bronchi and bronchioles caused by destruction of muscle and elastic tissue, resulting from or associated with chronic necrotising infection. Two processes are needed: obstruction and infection. Causes: congenital/hereditary (cystic fibrosis, primary ciliary dyskinesia/Kartagener syndrome, immunodeficiency), post-infectious (necrotising pneumonia — TB, measles, pertussis, adenovirus), bronchial obstruction (tumour, foreign body), allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis, and rheumatoid arthritis. Usually bilateral, lower lobes. Clinically: chronic cough with copious foul purulent sputum, haemoptysis, recurrent fever and clubbing. Complications: lung abscess, cor pulmonale, metastatic brain abscess and amyloidosis.",
[["Pathogenesis",["Obstruction → impaired clearance → infection","Infection → neutrophil proteases destroy wall → dilatation","Dilatation → further stasis → vicious cycle"]],
["Morphology",["Lower lobes, bilateral; airways dilated up to 4× normal, can be followed to the pleura","Cylindrical, fusiform, saccular forms","Acute and chronic inflammation, ulceration, squamous metaplasia, peribronchial fibrosis"]],
["Complications",["Recurrent pneumonia, abscess, empyema","Haemoptysis","Cor pulmonale","Brain abscess, secondary AA amyloidosis"]]],
{c:"Bronchiectasis is the end result of a cycle of obstruction and infection; postural drainage and antibiotics control it, and HRCT confirms the diagnosis."});

Z("path11",6,"p",["Pneumoconioses are lung diseases caused by inhaling mineral dusts at work, leading to inflammation and scarring.","The lungs' cleaning crew (macrophages) eats dust it cannot digest; the dust kills them and their dying signals cause scarring.",["Coal workers: anthracosis → simple CWP (coal macules) → progressive massive fibrosis.","Silicosis: silica in miners, sandblasters; upper-lobe nodules, eggshell calcification of nodes, ↑ TB risk.","Asbestosis: lower-lobe fibrosis, pleural plaques, ferruginous bodies; mesothelioma and lung cancer.","Particles 1–5 µm reach alveoli."],"Silica = Smoking TB risk & upper lobes; Asbestos = lower lobes & mesothelioma."],
"Pneumoconioses are non-neoplastic lung reactions to inhaled mineral dusts encountered at work. Disease depends on dust amount and retention, particle size (1–5 µm reach alveoli), solubility and reactivity. Coal workers' pneumoconiosis ranges from asymptomatic anthracosis to simple CWP (coal macules and nodules in upper lobes) and complicated CWP (progressive massive fibrosis); Caplan syndrome with rheumatoid arthritis. Silicosis is the commonest occupational disease worldwide (crystalline quartz; mining, sandblasting, stone cutting): silica-laden macrophages activate inflammasome and release mediators producing hyalinised whorled nodules in upper zones, eggshell calcification of hilar nodes and increased susceptibility to tuberculosis. Asbestosis causes diffuse interstitial fibrosis starting in lower lobes, with asbestos (ferruginous) bodies, pleural plaques and effusions, and increased risk of bronchogenic carcinoma (synergistic with smoking) and malignant mesothelioma.",
[["Pathogenesis",["Dust ingested by alveolar macrophages","Silica/asbestos activate NLRP3 inflammasome → IL-1, IL-18, ROS","Fibrogenic growth factors (TGF-β, PDGF) → fibroblast proliferation, collagen","Coal dust relatively inert; silica most fibrogenic"]],
["Comparison",[["Feature","Coal workers","Silicosis","Asbestosis"],["Occupation","Coal miners","Mining, sandblasting, stone cutting","Shipyards, insulation, brake linings"],["Zone","Upper","Upper","Lower"],["Lesion","Coal macules, PMF","Hyalinised whorled nodules","Diffuse interstitial fibrosis"],["Special","Caplan syndrome","Eggshell calcification, TB risk","Ferruginous bodies, pleural plaques"],["Cancer risk","Not increased","Lung cancer slightly ↑","Lung cancer, mesothelioma"]]],
["Clinical",["Progressive dyspnoea, restrictive pattern","Chest X-ray: nodules (silica), reticular opacities lower zones (asbestos)","Prevention: dust control, masks, wet drilling, periodic screening"]]],
{c:"Pneumoconioses are preventable occupational diseases; their distribution and markers (eggshell nodes, ferruginous bodies) help identify the dust responsible."});

Z("path11",7,"p",["Bronchogenic carcinoma is cancer arising from the lining of the airways, the leading cause of cancer death, mostly due to smoking.","Each cigarette chips away at the cell's 'brakes' (tumour suppressor genes) until one cell loses control.",["Types: adenocarcinoma (commonest, peripheral, non-smokers & women), squamous (central, smokers, PTH-rP → hypercalcaemia), small cell (central, very aggressive, ADH/ACTH), large cell.","Smoking risk depends on pack-years.","Paraneoplastic syndromes and Pancoast tumour (Horner syndrome).","Mutations: EGFR, ALK (adenocarcinoma, targetable), TP53, KRAS."],"Squamous = 'Sentral & Smoker & Stone (Ca²⁺)'; Small cell = 'Super aggressive, SIADH, Cushing'."],
"Bronchogenic carcinoma (lung cancer) is the leading cause of cancer deaths worldwide. About 90% are linked to smoking; other factors include radon, asbestos, arsenic, nickel, chromium, air pollution and genetic susceptibility. WHO types: adenocarcinoma (commonest; peripheral; women and non-smokers; EGFR, ALK, KRAS mutations), squamous cell carcinoma (central; strong smoking link; keratin pearls; hypercalcaemia from PTHrP), small cell carcinoma (central, neuroendocrine, very aggressive, early metastases, SIADH, ACTH, Lambert–Eaton) and large cell carcinoma. Clinical: cough, haemoptysis, weight loss, chest pain, dyspnoea, recurrent pneumonia, superior vena cava syndrome, Pancoast tumour with Horner syndrome, hoarseness. Spread: hilar nodes, adrenal, liver, brain, bone. Diagnosis: imaging, bronchoscopy with biopsy, sputum cytology; molecular testing guides targeted therapy.",
[["Etiology",["Cigarette smoking (dose-related; > 60 carcinogens such as polycyclic hydrocarbons, nitrosamines)","Occupational: asbestos (synergistic with smoking), radon, uranium, arsenic, nickel, chromium","Air pollution, previous lung scars","Genetic: TP53, RB, KRAS, EGFR, ALK, CDKN2A"]],
["Histological types",[["Type","Frequency","Site","Features"],["Adenocarcinoma","~40%","Peripheral","Glands, mucin; non-smokers, women; EGFR/ALK; adenocarcinoma in situ (lepidic)"],["Squamous cell","~25–30%","Central (hilar)","Keratin pearls, intercellular bridges; cavitation; PTHrP"],["Small cell","~15%","Central","Small oat-shaped cells, nuclear moulding, crush artefact; neuroendocrine markers"],["Large cell","~10%","Peripheral","Undifferentiated large cells"]]],
["Pathogenesis",["Field effect: squamous metaplasia → dysplasia → carcinoma in situ → invasive (squamous)","Atypical adenomatous hyperplasia → AIS → invasive adenocarcinoma","Accumulation of oncogene activation and tumour suppressor loss"]],
["Clinical features and local effects",["Cough, haemoptysis, dyspnoea, weight loss","Obstruction → collapse, pneumonia, abscess","Pancoast tumour (apex): Horner syndrome, brachial plexus pain","SVC syndrome; recurrent laryngeal nerve palsy (hoarseness); phrenic nerve palsy; pericardial and pleural effusion"]]],
{i:"Lung cancer incidence parallels smoking trends; in India it is the commonest cancer in men and increasing in women.",x:[["Paraneoplastic syndromes",[["Syndrome","Mediator","Type"],["Hypercalcaemia","PTHrP","Squamous"],["SIADH (hyponatraemia)","ADH","Small cell"],["Cushing syndrome","ACTH","Small cell"],["Lambert–Eaton","Antibodies to Ca²⁺ channels","Small cell"],["Hypertrophic osteoarthropathy, clubbing","—","Adeno, others"],["Trousseau (migratory thrombophlebitis)","Mucin","Adenocarcinoma"]]],["Spread and diagnosis",["Lymphatic: hilar, mediastinal, supraclavicular nodes","Blood: adrenal (50%), liver, brain, bone","Diagnosis: CXR/CT, bronchoscopy biopsy, sputum cytology, CT-guided FNAC, PET; EGFR/ALK/PD-L1 testing"]]],f:"Smoking → carcinogens → DNA damage (TP53, KRAS, EGFR)\n → metaplasia → dysplasia → CIS → invasive carcinoma\n → local spread (obstruction, SVC, Pancoast)\n → nodes → adrenal, liver, brain, bone\n → paraneoplastic syndromes",c:"Lung cancer is largely preventable by avoiding tobacco; histological typing and molecular profiling now drive personalised therapy."});

Z("path11",8,"p",["A lung abscess is a localised area of lung tissue destroyed by infection, leaving a pus-filled cavity.","Like a pocket of rotting tissue inside the lung that walls itself off.",["Commonest cause: aspiration of oral contents (alcoholics, unconscious, dental sepsis) — anaerobes.","Aspiration abscesses: right lung, posterior segment of upper lobe or apical segment of lower lobe.","Foul-smelling copious sputum, fever; cavity with air–fluid level on X-ray.","Other causes: Staph aureus, Klebsiella pneumonia, septic emboli, obstructing tumour."],"Abscess = Aspiration + Anaerobes + Air–fluid level."],
"A lung abscess is a localised area of suppurative necrosis within lung parenchyma resulting in one or more large cavities. Causes: aspiration of infective material (commonest: alcoholism, coma, anaesthesia, dental sepsis), complication of necrotising bacterial pneumonia (Staphylococcus aureus, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas), septic embolism (infective endocarditis of right heart), bronchial obstruction (carcinoma) and direct spread. Organisms are commonly mixed anaerobes (Bacteroides, Fusobacterium, Peptostreptococcus). Aspiration abscesses are commoner on the right (more vertical bronchus) and involve dependent segments. Abscesses range from millimetres to several centimetres, contain pus and may show air–fluid levels. Clinical: cough with copious foul sputum, fever, chest pain, clubbing. Complications: empyema, haemorrhage, brain abscess and amyloidosis.",
[["Causes",["Aspiration — anaerobes","Post-pneumonic — Staph aureus, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas","Septic emboli","Bronchial obstruction by tumour, foreign body"]],
["Morphology",["Single or multiple cavities with pus, ragged walls, surrounding inflammation and fibrosis","Air–fluid level if communicates with bronchus"]],
["Complications",["Rupture into pleura → empyema, bronchopleural fistula","Haemoptysis","Septic embolism → brain abscess, meningitis","Secondary amyloidosis"]]],
{c:"Most lung abscesses heal with prolonged antibiotics and drainage; persistent cavities in older smokers should raise suspicion of underlying carcinoma."});

Z("path11",9,"p",["Pulmonary tuberculosis is lung infection by Mycobacterium tuberculosis, causing granulomas with caseous necrosis; primary TB is the first infection, secondary TB the reactivation.","The body walls off the bacilli in granulomas like a prison; if immunity weakens, the prisoners break out (reactivation).",["Primary: Ghon focus (lower upper lobe/upper lower lobe) + hilar node = Ghon complex.","Secondary: apical cavitary lesions (Assmann/Simon focus), haemoptysis.","Histology: epithelioid cells, Langhans giant cells, caseation.","Spread: miliary TB to many organs."],"Primary = Ghon complex; Secondary = apex cavity."],
"Pulmonary tuberculosis is a chronic granulomatous infection caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis, transmitted by droplet nuclei. Primary tuberculosis occurs in an unsensitised host: a 1–1.5 cm Ghon focus forms in the subpleural lower upper lobe or upper lower lobe with caseation; bacilli drain to hilar lymph nodes, forming the Ghon complex, which usually heals with fibrosis and calcification (Ranke complex). In the immunocompromised it may progress (progressive primary TB). Secondary (post-primary) tuberculosis arises by reactivation or reinfection in a sensitised host, localised to the apex of one or both upper lobes, producing caseation and cavitation, with fibrocaseous disease, haemoptysis and spread through bronchi, lymphatics and blood (miliary TB: lungs, liver, spleen, kidney, meninges, marrow). The cell-mediated immune response (Th1, IFN-γ, macrophage activation) produces the granuloma: central caseous necrosis surrounded by epithelioid cells, Langhans giant cells, lymphocytes and fibroblasts.",
[["Primary vs secondary",[["Feature","Primary","Secondary"],["Host","Not sensitised","Previously sensitised"],["Site","Ghon focus (mid-lung, subpleural)","Apex of upper lobes"],["Lymph nodes","Prominently involved (Ghon complex)","Less involved"],["Cavitation","Rare","Common"],["Outcome","Heals (Ranke complex) / progressive","Progressive fibrocaseous, spread"]]],
["Pathogenesis",["Bacilli phagocytosed by alveolar macrophages; inhibit phagolysosome fusion and multiply","After ~3 weeks Th1 response: IL-12 → IFN-γ → macrophage activation → epithelioid cells, giant cells","TNF promotes granuloma; hypersensitivity → caseation","Tuberculin test positive"]],
["Morphology and spread",["Granuloma: caseous centre, epithelioid cells, Langhans giant cells (horseshoe nuclei), lymphocytes, fibrosis","Progressive pulmonary TB: cavity, erosion of vessels (Rasmussen aneurysm), bronchial spread","Miliary: millet-seed lesions in lungs and other organs","Tuberculous pleurisy, empyema, laryngeal and intestinal TB (swallowed sputum)"]]],
{c:"The tuberculous granuloma reflects cell-mediated immunity; its balance with bacillary multiplication determines whether TB heals, cavitates or disseminates."});

Z("path11",10,"p",["Pleural effusion is fluid in the pleural space; malignant mesothelioma is a cancer of the pleural lining linked to asbestos.","Pleura is a thin double raincoat around the lung; effusion is water trapped between its layers; mesothelioma is the raincoat itself turning cancerous and thick.",["Transudate: heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome. Exudate: TB, pneumonia, cancer, PE (Light's criteria).","Haemorrhagic effusion suggests malignancy, TB, infarction.","Mesothelioma: long latency (25–40 years) after asbestos; encases lung.","Histology: epithelioid, sarcomatoid or biphasic; calretinin and WT-1 positive."],"Asbestos → Pleural plaques → Mesothelioma (long wait)."],
"Pleural effusion is abnormal accumulation of fluid in the pleural cavity. Transudates (hydrothorax) arise from altered Starling forces (CHF, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome); exudates arise from inflammation or malignancy (pneumonia — parapneumonic, TB, metastatic cancer, mesothelioma, pulmonary infarction, SLE, RA). Light's criteria identify exudates (protein ratio > 0.5, LDH ratio > 0.6). Variants: empyema (pus), haemothorax (blood), chylothorax (lymph). Malignant mesothelioma is a rare highly malignant tumour of mesothelial cells, mainly pleural, strongly related to asbestos exposure (amphiboles) after 25–40 years latency. It spreads diffusely, encasing the lung in a thick white rind; histology epithelioid, sarcomatoid or biphasic; markers calretinin, WT1, cytokeratin 5/6, D2-40; BAP1 loss. Presents with chest pain, dyspnoea and recurrent effusion.",
[["Transudate vs exudate (Light's criteria)",[["Feature","Transudate","Exudate"],["Protein (fluid/serum)","< 0.5","> 0.5"],["LDH (fluid/serum)","< 0.6","> 0.6"],["Fluid LDH","< 2/3 upper normal","> 2/3 upper normal"],["Causes","CHF, cirrhosis, nephrotic","TB, pneumonia, cancer, PE"]]],
["Mesothelioma",["Asbestos exposure (shipyards, insulation); smoking does not add risk","Diffuse pleural thickening encasing lung; invades chest wall","Epithelioid (best prognosis), sarcomatoid, biphasic","IHC: calretinin, WT-1, CK5/6 positive; CEA, TTF-1 negative (unlike adenocarcinoma)","Prognosis poor (median survival ~1 year)"]]],
{c:"Analysis of pleural fluid separates transudates from exudates and may reveal malignancy; asbestos exposure history is the key to suspecting mesothelioma."});

Z("path12",0,"p",["Retinoblastoma is the commonest malignant eye tumour of children, caused by loss of both copies of the RB tumour suppressor gene.","The RB gene is a brake; a child with one faulty brake from birth needs only one more 'hit' to lose control (Knudson two-hit).",["Children < 5 years; 40% hereditary (often bilateral), 60% sporadic (unilateral).","White pupillary reflex (leukocoria, 'cat's eye'), squint.","Histology: small round blue cells, Flexner–Wintersteiner rosettes.","Hereditary cases risk osteosarcoma later."],"Retinoblastoma = 'Two hits, white eye, rosettes'."],
"Retinoblastoma is the most common primary intraocular malignancy of childhood, arising from neuroepithelial cells of the retina. It results from inactivation of both alleles of the RB gene on chromosome 13q14 (Knudson two-hit hypothesis). Hereditary cases (40%) carry a germline mutation, present earlier, are often bilateral or multifocal and are at risk of second tumours (osteosarcoma, pineoblastoma — trilateral retinoblastoma); sporadic cases (60%) are unilateral. Presentation: leukocoria (white reflex), strabismus, reduced vision, glaucoma, proptosis. Gross: white nodular tumour with calcification. Histology: sheets of small round cells with hyperchromatic nuclei, necrosis, calcification, Flexner–Wintersteiner rosettes and Homer Wright rosettes, fleurettes. Spread via optic nerve to brain and haematogenously.",
[["Genetics",["RB gene (13q14) controls G1→S transition by binding E2F","Hereditary: germline + somatic hit; bilateral, early, second cancers","Sporadic: two somatic hits; unilateral, later"]],
["Morphology",["Gross: chalky white, friable tumour with calcification; endophytic (into vitreous) or exophytic (subretinal)","Micro: small round blue cells, necrosis, dystrophic calcification","Flexner–Wintersteiner rosettes (central lumen), Homer Wright rosettes, fleurettes"]],
["Clinical and prognosis",["Leukocoria, squint, red painful eye","Spread: optic nerve → CNS; choroid → blood → bone marrow","Treatment: chemotherapy, laser, cryotherapy, enucleation; survival > 90% if intraocular"]]],
{c:"Retinoblastoma is the classic model of a tumour suppressor gene; early detection of leukocoria allows cure with preservation of life and often vision."});

Z("path12",1,"p",["Pleomorphic adenoma is the commonest benign salivary gland tumour, made of a mixture of epithelial and connective tissue-like elements.","Like a fruit cake with many different ingredients — glands, cartilage-like and mucoid material all in one tumour.",["Commonest in parotid gland; painless slow-growing mobile swelling.","'Mixed tumour': epithelial + myoepithelial cells in chondromyxoid stroma.","Incomplete capsule with finger-like projections → recurrence if just shelled out.","Rarely turns malignant (carcinoma ex pleomorphic adenoma)."],"Pleomorphic = 'many forms'; parotid; recurs if capsule ignored."],
"Pleomorphic adenoma (benign mixed tumour) is the most common salivary gland neoplasm (60% of parotid tumours), presenting as a painless, slow-growing, mobile swelling at the angle of the jaw in middle-aged adults. It arises from cells with both epithelial and myoepithelial differentiation; PLAG1 gene rearrangements are frequent. Grossly, it is a well-demarcated, rounded mass with an incomplete capsule and a grey-white, glistening, myxoid or cartilaginous cut surface. Microscopy shows ducts and acini lined by epithelial cells, sheets of myoepithelial cells, and a myxoid, chondroid or hyaline stroma. Tongue-like extensions through the capsule explain recurrence after enucleation; superficial parotidectomy is preferred. Malignant transformation (carcinoma ex pleomorphic adenoma) occurs in 2–10% of long-standing tumours.",
[["Morphology",["Gross: encapsulated (incompletely), lobulated, cut surface grey-white, myxoid, bluish cartilage-like areas","Micro: epithelial component (ducts, acini, squamous nests) + myoepithelial cells (spindle, plasmacytoid) + mesenchyme-like stroma (myxoid, chondroid, osseous)","Pseudopods penetrating capsule"]],
["Clinical",["Parotid (superficial lobe) most common; also submandibular, minor glands","Painless mobile swelling; facial nerve intact (pain/facial palsy suggests malignancy)","Recurrence 4–25% after enucleation","Treatment: superficial parotidectomy with nerve preservation"]],
["Comparison with Warthin tumour",[["Feature","Pleomorphic adenoma","Warthin tumour"],["Site","Parotid","Parotid only"],["Patient","Middle-aged, women","Older male smokers"],["Histology","Epithelial + stroma","Oncocytic bilayer + lymphoid stroma"],["Bilateral","Rare","10%"]]]],
{c:"Pleomorphic adenoma is benign but recurs if incompletely excised; adequate parotidectomy prevents recurrence and malignant change."});

Z("path12",2,"p",["Gastritis is inflammation of the stomach lining; acute gastritis is sudden and erosive, chronic gastritis is long-standing, mainly due to H. pylori or autoimmunity.","Like the stomach's protective paint peeling off — by drugs, alcohol, stress (acute) or by a long-term resident germ or immune attack (chronic).",["Acute: NSAIDs, alcohol, stress (Curling ulcers in burns, Cushing ulcers in head injury).","Chronic H. pylori gastritis: antrum, risk of peptic ulcer, adenocarcinoma, MALT lymphoma.","Autoimmune gastritis: body/fundus, anti-parietal cell and anti-intrinsic factor antibodies → pernicious anaemia.","Intestinal metaplasia is a precancerous change."],"H. pylori = Antrum; Autoimmune = Body (A for Antrum & Acid normal? B for B12 loss in Body)."],
"Gastritis is inflammation of the gastric mucosa. Acute gastritis results from disruption of mucosal defences by NSAIDs, alcohol, smoking, chemotherapy, uraemia, severe stress (burns — Curling ulcer; CNS injury — Cushing ulcer; shock, sepsis); it ranges from mild inflammation to haemorrhagic erosions. Chronic gastritis is most commonly due to Helicobacter pylori: antral-predominant gastritis with neutrophils, lymphoid follicles and plasma cells, associated with peptic ulcer, adenocarcinoma and MALT lymphoma. Autoimmune gastritis (< 10%) involves the body and fundus with antibodies to parietal cells (H⁺/K⁺-ATPase) and intrinsic factor, causing achlorhydria, hypergastrinaemia (G-cell hyperplasia), pernicious anaemia and increased risk of carcinoma and carcinoid. Both may progress to atrophy and intestinal metaplasia.",
[["Chronic gastritis types",[["Feature","H. pylori gastritis","Autoimmune gastritis"],["Location","Antrum","Body and fundus"],["Mechanism","Infection, urease, CagA","Anti-parietal cell & anti-IF antibodies"],["Acid","Normal/↑","↓ (achlorhydria)"],["Gastrin","Normal/↑","↑↑ (G-cell hyperplasia)"],["Associations","Peptic ulcer, adenocarcinoma, MALT lymphoma","Pernicious anaemia, carcinoid, adenocarcinoma"]]],
["Morphology",["Acute: oedema, congestion, neutrophils, erosions, haemorrhage","H. pylori: curved organisms in surface mucus, neutrophils in glands, lymphoid aggregates","Autoimmune: diffuse mucosal atrophy, loss of parietal cells, intestinal metaplasia"]],
["Diagnosis of H. pylori",["Biopsy (Giemsa/Warthin–Starry stain), rapid urease test","Urea breath test, stool antigen, serology"]]],
{c:"Chronic gastritis is a precursor lesion: eradication of H. pylori prevents ulcers and reduces cancer risk."});

Z("path12",3,"p",["A peptic ulcer is a breach in the stomach or duodenal lining extending through the muscularis mucosae, caused by acid and pepsin when defences are weak.","A balance scale: acid and pepsin attack on one side, mucus, bicarbonate and blood flow defend on the other — H. pylori and NSAIDs tip it.",["Commonest sites: first part of duodenum, lesser curve of stomach (antrum).","Causes: H. pylori (most), NSAIDs, smoking, Zollinger–Ellison syndrome.","Duodenal ulcer: pain relieved by food; gastric ulcer: pain worsened by food.","Complications: bleeding (commonest), perforation, obstruction, malignancy (gastric only)."],"Duodenal = Decreased pain with food; Gastric = Greater pain with food."],
"Peptic ulcer is a chronic, usually solitary mucosal ulceration (> 0.5 cm) penetrating at least to the submucosa, occurring in areas exposed to acid–peptic juice: first part of the duodenum, gastric antrum (lesser curvature), lower oesophagus, Meckel diverticulum and stomal margins. It results from imbalance between aggressive factors (acid, pepsin, H. pylori, NSAIDs, smoking, gastrinoma) and defensive factors (mucus–bicarbonate barrier, blood flow, prostaglandins, epithelial renewal). H. pylori causes 70–90% of duodenal ulcers. Gross: round to oval, punched-out ulcer with straight walls, flat margins and clean base; radiating mucosal folds. Microscopy shows four zones: necrotic debris, non-specific inflammation, granulation tissue, and fibrous scar. Complications: haemorrhage, perforation, gastric outlet obstruction, penetration into pancreas.",
[["Gastric vs duodenal ulcer",[["Feature","Gastric","Duodenal"],["Site","Lesser curvature, antrum","First part (bulb), anterior wall"],["Age","50–60","25–50"],["Acid","Normal or low","High"],["H. pylori","60–70%","~90%"],["Pain","Soon after meals, worse with food","2–3 h after meals, at night; relieved by food"],["Weight","Loss","Gain"],["Malignant risk","Must biopsy to exclude cancer","Practically never malignant"],["Complication","Bleeding, malignancy","Perforation, bleeding, obstruction"]]],
["Morphology",["Round, punched-out, 2–4 cm, sharply defined edges, clean base","Mucosal folds radiate from crater","Zones: necrotic fibrinoid debris → inflammation → granulation tissue → fibrosis (with thickened vessels)"]],
["Benign vs malignant gastric ulcer",[["Feature","Benign","Malignant"],["Edge","Flat, punched-out","Heaped up, rolled"],["Base","Clean","Irregular, necrotic"],["Folds","Radiate to edge","Interrupted, nodular"],["Size","Usually < 4 cm","Often larger"]]]],
{c:"Most peptic ulcers heal with acid suppression and H. pylori eradication; every gastric ulcer must be biopsied to exclude carcinoma."});

Z("path12",4,"p",["Inflammatory bowel disease is chronic immune-mediated gut inflammation: Crohn's disease can affect any part with skip lesions and transmural damage; ulcerative colitis is continuous mucosal disease of the colon starting from the rectum.","Crohn's is like scattered potholes that go through the road; UC is like a continuous sheet of surface damage spreading from one end.",["Crohn's: terminal ileum, skip lesions, cobblestone, fissures, fistulae, strictures, non-caseating granulomas.","UC: rectum upward, continuous, crypt abscesses, pseudopolyps, toxic megacolon.","UC: bloody diarrhoea; Crohn's: pain, malabsorption, fistulae.","UC has higher colon cancer risk; smoking worsens Crohn's but protects in UC."],"Crohn's = 'Cobblestones, Creeping fat, Crypts less hit, Can go anywhere'; UC = 'Up from rectum, Continuous'."],
"Inflammatory bowel disease is a chronic relapsing inflammatory condition resulting from inappropriate mucosal immune activation in genetically susceptible hosts (NOD2 in Crohn's) against commensal microbes, with epithelial barrier defects. Crohn disease may involve any part of the GI tract (mouth to anus), most often terminal ileum, with skip lesions, transmural inflammation, non-caseating granulomas, linear ulcers producing cobblestone mucosa, fissures, fistulae, strictures and creeping fat; it presents with diarrhoea, abdominal pain, fever and malabsorption. Ulcerative colitis is limited to the colon and rectum, starts in the rectum and extends proximally in a continuous manner, involving mucosa and submucosa only, with broad-based ulcers, pseudopolyps, crypt abscesses and crypt distortion; it presents with bloody mucoid diarrhoea. Extraintestinal features include arthritis, uveitis, erythema nodosum, pyoderma gangrenosum and primary sclerosing cholangitis (UC). Dysplasia and colorectal carcinoma risk rises with duration, especially in pancolitis.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Crohn's disease","Ulcerative colitis"],["Region","Ileum ± colon, any part","Colon only, rectum always"],["Distribution","Skip lesions","Continuous"],["Depth","Transmural","Mucosa and submucosa"],["Wall","Thick, 'garden-hose'","Thin"],["Ulcers","Deep, knife-like, linear","Superficial, broad-based"],["Cobblestone, fissures, fistulae","Present","Absent"],["Strictures","Common","Rare"],["Pseudopolyps","Moderate","Marked"],["Granulomas","Yes (35%)","No"],["Crypt abscesses","Present","Prominent"],["Toxic megacolon","Rare","Present"],["Cancer risk","Slight ↑","Markedly ↑"],["Serology","ASCA +","p-ANCA +"],["Smoking","Worsens","Protective"]]],
["Pathogenesis",["Genetic: NOD2 (Crohn's), HLA, ATG16L1, IL-23R","Epithelial barrier defects and abnormal response to commensal bacteria","Th1/Th17 (Crohn's) and Th2-like (UC) cytokines; TNF central","Environmental: Western diet, smoking, antibiotics (hygiene hypothesis)"]],
["Clinical features",["Crohn's: intermittent diarrhoea, pain in right iliac fossa, weight loss, fistulae, perianal disease, B12 deficiency","UC: bloody diarrhoea with mucus, tenesmus, lower abdominal cramps"]]],
{i:"IBD encompasses two related but distinct diseases; distinguishing them matters because surgery cures UC but not Crohn's disease.",x:[["Complications",["Crohn's: strictures with obstruction, fistulae (enterovesical, enterocutaneous), malabsorption, abscess","UC: toxic megacolon, perforation, massive bleeding, dysplasia → carcinoma","Extraintestinal: migratory polyarthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, uveitis, erythema nodosum, pyoderma gangrenosum, PSC (UC)"]]],f:"Genetic susceptibility + microbiome + barrier defect\n → abnormal mucosal immune response (TNF, IL-23/Th17)\n ├ Crohn's: transmural, granulomas, skip lesions → strictures, fistulae\n └ UC: mucosal, continuous from rectum → bloody diarrhoea, megacolon, cancer",c:"Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis share pathogenic mechanisms but differ in distribution, depth and complications; colonoscopic biopsy distinguishes them and guides surveillance for cancer."});

Z("path12",5,"p",["Intestinal tuberculosis usually affects the ileocaecal region; enteric (typhoid) fever ulcerates the Peyer's patches of the terminal ileum.","TB ulcers run around the bowel like rings (transverse, along lymphatics); typhoid ulcers run along the bowel (longitudinal, over Peyer's patches).",["Secondary intestinal TB: swallowed sputum from open lung TB.","TB ulcers: transverse (girdle), strictures heal by fibrosis.","Typhoid: oval longitudinal ulcers over Peyer's patches; perforation in 3rd week.","Typhoid: Salmonella Typhi; Widal test; blood culture in week 1."],"TB = Transverse; Typhoid = 'Tall' (longitudinal)."],
"Intestinal tuberculosis is primary (ingestion of infected milk — M. bovis, rare now) or secondary (swallowing of bacilli from open pulmonary TB). The ileocaecal region is commonest owing to abundant lymphoid tissue and stasis. Forms: ulcerative (transverse/girdle ulcers along lymphatics, undermined edges), hypertrophic (caseous mass mimicking cancer) and ulcerohypertrophic; healing by fibrosis causes strictures and obstruction. Histology shows caseating granulomas. Enteric fever is caused by Salmonella Typhi and Paratyphi; bacilli invade Peyer's patches, causing hyperplasia, necrosis and oval ulcers with long axis along the bowel in the terminal ileum; perforation and haemorrhage occur in the third week. Microscopy: macrophages containing bacilli, RBCs and lymphocytes (typhoid nodules), with scanty neutrophils. Healing does not cause stricture.",
[["Comparison of ulcers",[["Feature","Tuberculous","Typhoid"],["Site","Ileocaecal","Terminal ileum (Peyer's patches)"],["Axis","Transverse (girdle)","Longitudinal (oval)"],["Edges","Undermined, irregular","Raised, regular"],["Histology","Caseating granulomas","Macrophages (typhoid nodules), no neutrophils"],["Healing","Fibrosis → stricture","No stricture"],["Complications","Obstruction, malabsorption","Perforation, haemorrhage (3rd week)"]]],
["Diagnosis",["Intestinal TB: colonoscopic biopsy, AFB stain, CBNAAT, culture; CT abdomen","Typhoid: blood culture (week 1), Widal (week 2), stool/urine culture (week 3), bone marrow culture"]]],
{c:"The orientation of ulcers and their histology distinguish tuberculous from typhoid ulceration; both are common causes of small bowel perforation and obstruction in India."});

Z("path12",6,"p",["Malabsorption syndrome is failure of the gut to absorb nutrients properly, causing fatty stools, weight loss and deficiencies.","Like a sieve that has lost its fine mesh (villi) or lacks the detergent (bile/enzymes) to break down food.",["Steatorrhoea: pale, bulky, foul, greasy stools.","Causes: coeliac disease, tropical sprue, chronic pancreatitis, bile deficiency, Whipple disease, lactase deficiency, giardiasis.","Coeliac: anti-tTG antibodies, villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia; gluten-free diet.","Effects: anaemia, osteomalacia, bleeding (vit K), oedema."],"Malabsorption = 'Fat in stool, Fall in weight, Few vitamins'."],
"Malabsorption syndrome is defective absorption of fats, fat-soluble and other vitamins, proteins, carbohydrates, electrolytes and water. Mechanisms: defective intraluminal digestion (pancreatic insufficiency — chronic pancreatitis, cystic fibrosis; bile salt deficiency — obstruction, ileal resection, bacterial overgrowth), primary mucosal disease (coeliac disease, tropical sprue, lactase deficiency, Whipple disease), reduced surface area (short bowel), lymphatic obstruction (lymphangiectasia, lymphoma) and infections (giardiasis). Clinical: chronic diarrhoea, steatorrhoea, weight loss, flatulence, anaemia (iron, folate, B12), bleeding (vitamin K), osteomalacia (vitamin D), hypoalbuminaemic oedema. Coeliac disease is gluten-sensitive enteropathy (HLA-DQ2/DQ8) with anti-tissue transglutaminase antibodies, villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia and intraepithelial lymphocytosis, reversing on a gluten-free diet.",
[["Causes",[["Mechanism","Examples"],["Intraluminal","Chronic pancreatitis, cystic fibrosis, bile obstruction, bacterial overgrowth"],["Mucosal","Coeliac disease, tropical sprue, lactase deficiency, Whipple disease"],["Reduced surface","Short bowel syndrome, Crohn's"],["Lymphatic","Intestinal lymphangiectasia, lymphoma"],["Infections","Giardia, Cryptosporidium, HIV enteropathy"]]],
["Investigations",["Faecal fat estimation (> 7 g/day), Sudan III stain","D-xylose test: mucosal vs pancreatic cause","Serology: anti-tTG IgA, anti-endomysial","Small bowel biopsy: villous atrophy","Lactose breath test"]],
["Coeliac disease morphology",["Villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia","Increased intraepithelial lymphocytes, lamina propria plasma cells","Distal duodenum and proximal jejunum"]]],
{c:"A careful work-up separates luminal, mucosal and lymphatic causes of malabsorption; coeliac disease is the commonest treatable mucosal cause."});

Z("path12",7,"p",["Carcinoid tumour is a slow-growing neuroendocrine tumour, commonly in the appendix and small intestine, that can secrete serotonin.","Like a small hormone factory hidden in the gut; its products cause symptoms only when they bypass the liver's filter (metastasis).",["Commonest site: small intestine (ileum) and appendix.","Yellow, firm submucosal nodule; nests of uniform cells with 'salt-and-pepper' chromatin.","Carcinoid syndrome: flushing, diarrhoea, bronchospasm, right-sided valve fibrosis — usually with liver metastases.","Diagnosis: urine 5-HIAA, chromogranin A."],"Carcinoid = 'Serotonin → Flush, Flush (diarrhoea), Wheeze, Right heart'."],
"Carcinoid tumours (well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumours) arise from neuroendocrine cells, most commonly in the small intestine (ileum), appendix, rectum, stomach and lungs. They are yellow-tan, firm, submucosal intramural masses; microscopically islands, trabeculae or glands of uniform cells with scant pink granular cytoplasm and round nuclei with salt-and-pepper chromatin, positive for chromogranin and synaptophysin. Appendiceal carcinoids are usually benign; ileal tumours metastasise more often. Carcinoid syndrome (< 10%), caused by serotonin, bradykinin and histamine reaching systemic circulation (usually from hepatic metastases), features cutaneous flushing, diarrhoea, abdominal cramps, bronchospasm and right-sided endocardial fibrosis (tricuspid and pulmonary valves). Diagnosis by urinary 5-HIAA, serum chromogranin A and somatostatin receptor imaging.",
[["Features",["Sites: small bowel > appendix > rectum > stomach > bronchus","Gross: yellow submucosal nodule, may cause intussusception or obstruction; desmoplastic mesenteric reaction","Micro: nests, trabeculae, rosettes; salt-and-pepper chromatin; argentaffin positive","IHC: chromogranin A, synaptophysin, CD56"]],
["Carcinoid syndrome",["Flushing, diarrhoea, bronchospasm, cyanosis","Right heart valve fibrosis (left heart protected by lung MAO)","Needs hepatic metastasis (bypasses liver first-pass) or extraintestinal primary","Urinary 5-HIAA ↑"]],
["Prognosis","Depends on site, size (> 2 cm), depth, Ki-67 grade; appendix best, ileum worse."]],
{c:"Carcinoids are indolent neuroendocrine tumours; the carcinoid syndrome signals metastatic disease and is treated with somatostatin analogues."});

Z("path12",8,"p",["Colorectal carcinoma is cancer of the large bowel, usually an adenocarcinoma arising from a pre-existing adenomatous polyp.","A small polyp is a seed; with each new genetic 'hit' (APC → KRAS → p53) it grows into a full cancer.",["Risk: age > 50, low-fibre high-fat diet, family history, FAP, Lynch syndrome, IBD.","Adenoma–carcinoma sequence; MSI pathway in Lynch syndrome.","Right-sided: anaemia, mass, fungating; left-sided: obstruction, napkin-ring, change in bowel habit.","Staging: TNM / modified Dukes; CEA for follow-up."],"Right = 'Rich in blood loss (anaemia)'; Left = 'Lumen blocked (obstruction)'."],
"Colorectal carcinoma is one of the commonest cancers worldwide, 98% adenocarcinoma, peaking at 60–70 years. Risk factors: age, diet (low fibre, high red meat and fat), obesity, smoking, alcohol, family history, inherited syndromes (familial adenomatous polyposis — APC; Lynch syndrome/HNPCC — mismatch repair genes), and long-standing IBD. Pathogenesis follows the APC/β-catenin adenoma–carcinoma sequence (APC → KRAS → SMAD4 → TP53) or the microsatellite instability pathway. Right-sided cancers are polypoid, exophytic, causing iron-deficiency anaemia, fatigue and a mass; left-sided cancers are annular 'napkin-ring' lesions causing obstruction, altered bowel habit and bleeding per rectum. Histology: tall columnar cells forming glands with desmoplasia, dirty necrosis; some mucinous or signet-ring. Spread to nodes and liver. Prognosis depends on stage (TNM/Dukes); CEA monitors recurrence.",
[["Etiology",["Diet: low fibre, high red/processed meat, high fat","Adenomatous polyps (villous, > 2 cm, high-grade dysplasia = higher risk)","FAP (100% cancer risk), Lynch syndrome","Ulcerative colitis, Crohn's colitis","Age > 50, obesity, smoking, alcohol"]],
["Pathogenesis",[["Pathway","Genes","Features"],["APC/β-catenin (80%)","APC → KRAS → SMAD4 → TP53","Adenoma–carcinoma sequence; chromosomal instability"],["Microsatellite instability (10–15%)","MLH1, MSH2 (mismatch repair)","Lynch syndrome, right-sided, mucinous, lymphocytes"],["CpG island methylator","BRAF","Serrated polyp pathway"]]],
["Morphology and clinical",[["Feature","Right colon","Left colon/rectum"],["Gross","Polypoid, exophytic","Annular, napkin-ring, constricting"],["Presentation","Anaemia, weakness, mass","Obstruction, altered bowel habit, bleeding PR"],["Lumen","Wide → late obstruction","Narrow → early obstruction"]]]],
{x:[["Staging (modified Dukes/TNM)",["A: confined to mucosa/submucosa (T1–2), > 90% 5-yr survival","B: through muscularis propria (T3–4), no nodes","C: lymph node metastases","D: distant metastases (liver commonest)"]],["Diagnosis",["Colonoscopy and biopsy; faecal occult blood screening","CT for staging; CEA baseline and follow-up"]]],c:"Colorectal carcinoma develops through a well-defined sequence from adenoma; screening colonoscopy with polypectomy can prevent it."});

Z("path12",9,"p",["Leukoplakia is a white patch in the mouth that cannot be rubbed off or explained otherwise; it may be a precursor of oral squamous cell carcinoma, which is very common in India due to tobacco.","White patch = warning light; some of them become cancer, especially red-white (speckled) ones.",["Leukoplakia: clinical term; histology ranges from hyperkeratosis to dysplasia.","Erythroplakia (red patch) has a much higher risk of malignancy.","Oral cancer risk factors: tobacco chewing (gutkha, pan), smoking, alcohol, HPV, betel quid.","India: buccal mucosa (gingivobuccal) commonest site; keratin pearls on histology."],"Leukoplakia = white warning; Erythroplakia = red danger."],
"Oral leukoplakia is a clinical term for a white patch or plaque on the oral mucosa that cannot be scraped off and cannot be characterised as any other disease. It occurs mostly in men aged 40–70 using tobacco and alcohol; histology varies from hyperkeratosis and acanthosis to dysplasia and carcinoma in situ; 5–25% are premalignant. Erythroplakia (red velvety patch) shows dysplasia in most cases and has > 50% risk of malignant change. Other premalignant conditions: oral submucous fibrosis (areca nut), lichen planus. Oral squamous cell carcinoma accounts for > 90% of oral cancers and is among the commonest cancers in Indian men; risk factors are tobacco (chewed and smoked), betel quid, alcohol and HPV (oropharynx). Common sites in India are buccal mucosa and gingivobuccal sulcus; lesions are ulceroproliferative or indurated ulcers. Histology: invasive nests of squamous cells with keratin pearls. Spread to cervical lymph nodes.",
[["Premalignant lesions",[["Lesion","Malignant potential"],["Homogeneous leukoplakia","Low (3–5%)"],["Speckled leukoplakia","Higher"],["Erythroplakia","Very high (> 50%)"],["Oral submucous fibrosis","~7%"]]],
["Oral SCC",["Risk: tobacco (khaini, gutkha), betel quid, smoking, alcohol, HPV 16","Sites: buccal mucosa (India), lateral tongue, floor of mouth (West)","Gross: ulceroproliferative growth with indurated everted edges","Micro: well to poorly differentiated keratinising SCC, keratin pearls, intercellular bridges","Spread: submandibular and deep cervical nodes; distant late"]]],
{c:"Recognising and biopsying white or red oral patches in tobacco users allows early detection of oral cancer, which is largely preventable by quitting tobacco."});

Z("path12",10,"p",["Barrett oesophagus is replacement of the normal squamous lining of the lower oesophagus by intestinal-type glandular lining due to long-term acid reflux; achalasia is failure of the lower oesophageal sphincter to relax.","Barrett: the oesophagus 'puts on the stomach's raincoat' to survive acid. Achalasia: the exit door is stuck shut, so food piles up behind it.",["Barrett: GERD → intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells → risk of adenocarcinoma.","Achalasia: loss of myenteric ganglion cells; dysphagia to solids and liquids.","Achalasia X-ray: 'bird-beak' narrowing with dilated oesophagus.","Chagas disease can cause secondary achalasia."],"Barrett = Bad reflux → Becomes glandular → adenocarcinoma; Achalasia = 'A-chalasia' = no relaxation."],
"Barrett oesophagus is a complication of chronic gastro-oesophageal reflux disease in which the stratified squamous epithelium of the distal oesophagus is replaced by metaplastic columnar epithelium with goblet cells (intestinal metaplasia). It is commonest in white middle-aged males. It is a precursor of oesophageal adenocarcinoma through low- and high-grade dysplasia; surveillance endoscopy with biopsy is required. Achalasia is a motility disorder with incomplete relaxation of the lower oesophageal sphincter, increased LES tone and aperistalsis of the oesophageal body, due to loss of inhibitory neurons (nitric oxide/VIP) in the myenteric plexus (primary/idiopathic) or secondary to Chagas disease, malignancy or amyloidosis. Patients have progressive dysphagia for both solids and liquids, regurgitation and aspiration; barium swallow shows a dilated oesophagus with bird-beak tapering. It carries a small risk of squamous carcinoma.",
[["Barrett oesophagus",["Cause: chronic GERD; risk factors obesity, smoking, hiatus hernia","Gross: red velvety mucosa extending up from GE junction","Micro: columnar epithelium with goblet cells (Alcian blue positive)","Progression: metaplasia → dysplasia → adenocarcinoma (30–40× risk)"]],
["Achalasia",["Triad: incomplete LES relaxation, ↑ LES tone, aperistalsis","Loss of myenteric ganglion cells (inhibitory neurons)","Secondary: Chagas (Trypanosoma cruzi), malignancy (pseudoachalasia)","Dysphagia (solids + liquids), regurgitation, weight loss","Barium: bird-beak; manometry diagnostic","Treatment: pneumatic dilatation, Heller myotomy, POEM, botulinum toxin"]]],
{c:"Barrett oesophagus is a metaplastic response to acid and the main precursor of oesophageal adenocarcinoma; achalasia is a neuromuscular failure presenting with dysphagia for both solids and liquids."});

Z("path12",11,"p",["Oesophageal carcinoma is squamous (middle third, tobacco, alcohol) or adenocarcinoma (lower third, Barrett). Gastric carcinoma is usually adenocarcinoma, intestinal or diffuse type.","Oesophageal cancer slowly narrows the food pipe; diffuse gastric cancer turns the stomach into a rigid leather bottle (linitis plastica).",["Oesophageal SCC: India, middle third; tobacco, alcohol, hot beverages, achalasia, Plummer–Vinson.","Adenocarcinoma: lower third, Barrett, obesity, GERD.","Gastric cancer: H. pylori, nitrosamines, smoked food, atrophic gastritis.","Intestinal type (glands, older men) vs diffuse type (signet-ring cells, linitis plastica, CDH1)."],"Gastric spread: Virchow node (left supraclavicular), Krukenberg (ovary), Sister Mary Joseph (umbilicus)."],
"Oesophageal carcinoma presents with progressive dysphagia for solids, weight loss and odynophagia. Squamous cell carcinoma (commonest in India) arises in the middle third and is linked to tobacco, alcohol, hot beverages, betel nut, achalasia, Plummer–Vinson syndrome, nutritional deficiency and HPV; adenocarcinoma arises in the distal third from Barrett oesophagus. Gastric carcinoma, mostly adenocarcinoma, is associated with H. pylori, dietary nitrosamines and smoked foods, chronic atrophic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, partial gastrectomy and pernicious anaemia. Lauren classification: intestinal type (glandular, older men, follows metaplasia) and diffuse type (signet ring cells infiltrating the wall, linitis plastica, younger patients, E-cadherin CDH1 loss). Spread: lymph nodes (Virchow node), liver, peritoneum, ovaries (Krukenberg tumour), umbilicus (Sister Mary Joseph nodule).",
[["Oesophageal carcinoma",[["Feature","Squamous cell","Adenocarcinoma"],["Site","Middle third","Lower third"],["Risk","Tobacco, alcohol, hot drinks, achalasia, Plummer–Vinson, HPV","Barrett oesophagus, GERD, obesity"],["Region","Asia, India","West"],["Histology","Keratin pearls","Glands, mucin"]]],
["Gastric carcinoma — Lauren types",[["Feature","Intestinal","Diffuse"],["Precursor","Intestinal metaplasia, H. pylori","No obvious precursor; CDH1"],["Histology","Glands","Signet ring cells, no glands"],["Gross","Polypoid/ulcerated mass","Linitis plastica (leather bottle)"],["Patient","Older men","Younger, both sexes"]]],
["Spread of gastric cancer",["Lymph nodes: perigastric, Virchow node (left supraclavicular, Troisier sign)","Liver; peritoneal seeding","Krukenberg tumour (bilateral ovarian signet ring metastases)","Sister Mary Joseph nodule (umbilicus)"]]],
{c:"Both oesophageal and gastric carcinomas present late and carry poor prognosis; tobacco avoidance, H. pylori eradication and surveillance of precursor lesions are key preventive measures."});

Z("path12",12,"p",["Four common bowel conditions: appendicitis (inflamed appendix), Hirschsprung disease (missing nerve cells in the colon), intussusception (bowel telescoping into itself), Meckel diverticulum (remnant of the vitello-intestinal duct).","Appendicitis: a blocked dead-end tube getting infected. Hirschsprung: a motor that never switched on. Intussusception: a sock folding into itself.",["Appendicitis: obstruction by faecolith → neutrophils in muscularis propria; McBurney point pain.","Hirschsprung: absent ganglion cells in distal colon → megacolon proximal; delayed meconium.","Intussusception: infants, red-currant jelly stool, sausage mass; adults — tumour lead point.","Meckel rule of 2s: 2% population, 2 feet from ileocaecal valve, 2 inches long, 2 types of ectopic tissue."],"Meckel = Rule of 2s; Hirschsprung = Hole in ganglia."],
"Acute appendicitis follows luminal obstruction (faecolith, lymphoid hyperplasia), with bacterial invasion and ischaemia; diagnosis rests on neutrophilic infiltration of the muscularis propria; it may progress to gangrene, perforation and peritonitis. Hirschsprung disease (congenital aganglionic megacolon) results from failure of neural crest cell migration (RET mutations, Down syndrome), leaving an aganglionic distal segment (always rectum) that cannot relax, with proximal dilatation; neonates fail to pass meconium; rectal biopsy shows absent ganglion cells and hypertrophied nerves (calretinin negative). Intussusception is telescoping of a proximal bowel segment into a distal one, commonest in infants (ileocolic, lymphoid hyperplasia after viral infection) and in adults with a tumour lead point; it causes colicky pain, sausage-shaped mass and red-currant jelly stool. Meckel diverticulum is a true diverticulum from persistent vitelline duct, on the antimesenteric ileum, containing ectopic gastric or pancreatic tissue; it causes bleeding, obstruction or diverticulitis.",
[["Summary",[["Condition","Key pathology","Presentation"],["Acute appendicitis","Neutrophils in muscularis propria; obstruction","Periumbilical → RIF pain, fever, McBurney tenderness"],["Hirschsprung","Absent Meissner and Auerbach ganglia distally","Failure to pass meconium, abdominal distension"],["Intussusception","Telescoping, venous congestion → infarction","Colic, red-currant jelly stool, sausage mass"],["Meckel diverticulum","True diverticulum; ectopic gastric mucosa","Painless rectal bleeding in children"]]],
["Complications",["Appendicitis: perforation, abscess, pylephlebitis","Hirschsprung: enterocolitis, perforation","Intussusception: gangrene, perforation","Meckel: bleeding, obstruction, diverticulitis"]]],
{c:"These common surgical conditions are recognised by characteristic clinicopathological features; timely diagnosis prevents gangrene and perforation."});
